Original commit body from e748b3578e9575d66ee5f351d9aa358ec0865313:

stamp_symbol_fingerprints caches one parsed tree per (path, language), so each
file is PARSED once. But the per-symbol step located the smallest AST node
covering a symbol's span with a fresh ast.walk over the whole module, so the
pass cost symbols(file) x nodes(file) — quadratic in file size, a large file
expensive twice over: once for having many AST nodes, again for having many
symbols that each rescan them. The tree cache had removed repeated PARSING but
not repeated LOCATION.

Measured 324.3s of a 517.7s cold run_survey on this monorepo — 62.6% of wall —
in a post-pass that carries no AnalysisRun card. That is why the time was
invisible in analysis_runs[] and had only ever been reached by subtracting pass
durations from the wall clock, which localises nothing.

The locator now bisects a per-tree index bucketed by effective start line and
scans start lines descending, breaking once the best extent reachable at the
current start line is worse than the incumbent: every covering node has
n_end >= end_line, so a node starting at n_start cannot have extent below
end_line - n_start, and that bound only grows as n_start shrinks.

  stamp_symbol_fingerprints   324.3s -> 22.8s   (14.2x)
  cold run_survey wall        517.7s -> 220.0s  (2.35x)

SEMANTICS PRESERVED EXACTLY, which is the binding constraint because a
fingerprint is serialized output. The candidate set is unchanged and the winner
is the lexicographic minimum of (extent, walk_ordinal), reproducing the old
strict < against ast.walk's breadth-first order — so on an equal-extent tie the
OUTER node still wins. Descending to the deepest covering node, the tempting
rewrite, inverts that tie and would silently rehash a different subtree; the
test pins the tie and ships a positive control proving the reference
implementation can distinguish the two rules.

Verified at repo scale, not only in unit tests: comparing every fingerprint in
the before and after surveys, 1 of 42,543 changed, and it is the file-level
fingerprint of fingerprint.py itself. Node-set deltas are confined to the files
this commit touches.

Scope expansion checked and negative: _tree_sitter_context_fingerprint locates
via descendant_for_byte_range (native O(depth) descent) and never scanned the
whole tree. The hand-written python walk was the one that went quadratic.

Three instruments land with the fix, since the earlier apportionment-by-
subtraction is what let this hide:
 - measure-survey-phase-split.py: times run_survey's phase functions with
   inclusive/exclusive accounting so shares sum to wall clock and the remainder
   prints as UNATTRIBUTED; a --lean arm prices the side outputs (refuting the
   standing hypothesis: side outputs are 62.7s, serialization 3.0s).
 - measure-fingerprint-stamp-cost.py: measures the locator across the real file
   size range (ns/node flat at ~495, the signature of a full-tree scan) and
   PREDICTS the pass total from production's symbols-per-file distribution, so a
   hot spot must explain the number it is blamed for.
 - compare-survey-fingerprints.py: survey-to-survey fingerprint differ.

5,718 passed, 100% coverage, mypy strict ratchet clean.

